  <data key="d3">The statement that this is a joint life insurance policy sets the context for why proof of both deaths is required—the policy's survival-based structure means benefits are contingent on the simultaneous passing of both individuals.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXEMPLIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ac80f582-a994-43e3-a0c9-7da66f0fc96b" target="70d6f164-2c6e-4960-9154-c1e13245d12c">
  <data key="d3">The policy is a joint life insurance contract designed to provide coverage for two individuals under a single agreement. It serves an estate planning function, specifically to generate funds needed to cover potential estate taxes when both insured parties pass away.</data>
  <data key="d4">INTENDED_FOR</data>
