<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="ac80f582-a994-43e3-a0c9-7da66f0fc96b">
  <data key="d3">The condition that the death benefit is payable only after the second insured's death defines what makes the policy "joint" under a survivorship model, reinforcing the foundational concept introduced in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="f7b2d174-40c9-40e2-a0f1-fafef35a9501">
  <data key="d3">Since the death benefit is contingent on both insured individuals passing away, the policy must cover two people from the outset, establishing the foundational premise for this payout condition.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="43d8caa1-4fa2-4bcc-9598-cb51dcf031e8">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 explains that the payout occurs solely after the second death, which is consistent with how a survivorship-based policy operates by deferring benefits until both parties have died.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="45022529-c9d1-42d4-978a-0e7ff593425f">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 specifies that the death benefit is contingent on the second death, which implies that no claim arises from the first death alone. This supports and explains why fact_sentence_2 states that no benefit is paid if only the first insured dies.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="e3ba5ff9-a204-4af4-850d-38d792f8f557">
  <data key="d3">Since the death benefit is only triggered by the second death, the policy must remain active post-first-death to ensure eligibility. Thus, continuation of coverage ensures compliance with the survival-based payout clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="5f7b886a-0895-4321-a983-5dcc79a03f70">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit be paid only after the second insured dies ensures that funds are available precisely when needed for post-death financial obligations typical in estate planning.</data>
  <data key="d4">SUPPORTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="bfe9a0f3-fce1-4c16-a4f6-2a78f6d39acf">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 specifies that payment occurs upon the second death, which aligns with fact_sentence_2's assertion that both deaths must occur before any payout. This reinforces the co-dependent nature of the triggering event.</data>
  <data key="d4">EQUIVALENT_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="70d6f164-2c6e-4960-9154-c1e13245d12c">
  <data key="d3">The second-to-die payout structure ensures that funds become available precisely when required for estate tax obligations, aligning the timing of the death benefit with post-death financial needs.</data>
  <data key="d4">SUPPORTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="30aa32c7-4f8f-42cf-99bf-592ed9d2eaab">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that both insureds must pass away before any payment occurs ensures that the $2.5 million benefit is issued solely upon confirmation of the second death. This triggers the insurer's obligation to disburse funds promptly once verified.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.97</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="00523b0d-56f9-449b-a7f2-e7f02fe9d7d8">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to submit proof of both deaths arises specifically because the death benefit is contingent on the second death occurring. This ensures claims are validated according to the policy's survivorship terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS_CLAIM</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="69f86d86-8dc3-4e5c-963e-490edef23e72">
  <data key="d3">The death benefit is only paid after the second insured dies, creating the condition under which any loan deductions would apply. This ensures that reductions from outstanding loans occur only if the claim becomes effective post-second death.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS PAYMENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="58074012-c329-4400-8d8b-4167293c8593">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to submit a death certificate for the second insured ensures verification of the event triggering the death benefit payment described in the first sentence.</data>
  <data key="d4">REQUIRED_FOR</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="8bb98441-7fce-4a4a-aaed-255a79d72dc9">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to submit a death certificate for the first insured is an essential preliminary step in initiating the claims process under the survivorship policy. Without confirming the first death, the insurer cannot proceed to verify the second death required for payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">PRECEDES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="b139e470-7b32-4675-85c3-cec60a1f5146">
  <data key="d3">The death benefit is contingent on both insureds passing away, but payment requires administrative action: submitting complete claim forms to initiate processing.</data>
  <data key="d4">REQUIRES_PROCESSING</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="539e1a9d-20b3-4a9d-906a-7f04bb12c255">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit be paid only after the second insured passes sets the precondition for claiming benefits. This triggers the 30-day processing window specified in fact_sentence_2, ensuring timely disbursement once all necessary documents are received.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS PAYMENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="4f649562-2015-4f25-ae81-7c5444b996c4">
  <data key="d3">Since the death benefit is only paid after the second death, the policy must remain active after the first death to fulfill this requirement. Therefore, the continuation of the policy ensures eligibility for future claims based on the survival order.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="b917e56c-f863-4041-b852-b34740f1b439">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit is paid only after the second death necessitates continued financial commitment through premium payments after the first death, ensuring the policy remains in force until its intended payout event.</data>
  <data key="d4">NECESSITATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="e11b535f-c978-4ee3-a0fb-67b135406066">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 states that the death benefit is triggered only after the second death, which explains why no benefit is paid when the first insured passes away—a key administrative outcome described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="25f4e64b-7adb-4f44-892f-2d9bd49fdc83">
  <data key="d3">The death benefit depends solely on the second death occurring, regardless of prior notifications about the first death. Confirmation of the first death serves an administrative purpose but does not alter the conditions under which the benefit is paid.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="a4c41a3b-9c7f-4b98-a6b4-986121ed83de">
  <data key="d3">When the first insured dies, the policy does not pay out but continues in force. The surviving insured's status is reclassified into a single-life whole life policy for administrative continuity.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="a9ec20a6-7d7d-487c-a092-216f40615962">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to file the first insured’s death certificate is a prerequisite for initiating the claims process, ensuring that the sequence of events leading to the second death can be verified. This procedural step enables the insurer to confirm eligibility for the death benefit tied to the second death.</data>
  <data key="d4">PRECEDES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="fe6ae23c-acf1-4ce0-b10a-d43f8f1cd48e">
  <data key="d3">Since the death benefit is only triggered by the second death, the policy's face value must stay constant until that event, ensuring the promised $2.5 million is available when required.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="5cdd5a6a-21a2-42b9-9024-4e7d8ff292a2">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit be paid only after the second insured dies defines the claim trigger, while the guaranteed-level premium ensures predictable costs over the first ten years regardless of when claims occur.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="9ff1d8f5-8894-4692-b73d-1e4ea24edfc5">
  <data key="d3">The death benefit is contingent solely on the second death occurring, ensuring payment only when both insured parties have passed. After the first death, the premium stays fixed, maintaining cost predictability even though the policy's nature shifts toward final settlement.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="e3c7d76f-bcfb-4c2d-988d-e3fa8983ae3e">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit is paid only after the second insured dies defines the claim eligibility condition. This structural feature does not influence when or how much the premium increases after Year 10.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="b9253ffc-7436-4d11-a036-5b41d8c8e66d">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit is paid only after the second death necessitates ongoing premium payments post-first-death to maintain coverage validity. Without continued premiums, the policy would lapse before the final payout event.</data>
  <data key="d4">NECESSITATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="3d46018a-fc19-4870-ba86-8b6a02b20a34">
  <data key="d3">The death benefit is contingent solely on the second death occurring, regardless of when premiums are paid. Premium payments occur annually on a fixed date but do not influence or depend on the triggering event for the benefit.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="8aef280a-0c9a-40d3-968e-a753e7c3402f">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence defines when the death benefit is paid, specifically requiring both insureds to pass away. This timing criterion has no bearing on the premium cap established in the second sentence, which governs cost limits beyond Year 10 regardless of claim triggers.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="0e404f06-62de-4907-9ecb-1ee9ca03ea3f">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 clarifies the payment trigger—only after the second insured passes—which fully explains why the policy in fact_sentence_1 is classified as Second-to-Die Whole Life, validating its structural logic.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="2d075601-ee57-47c1-9e7a-50f0b163454b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 clarifies the payout timing dependent on the second death, which explains why fact_sentence_1 identifies it as a joint policy designed specifically for such a scenario.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="3f6d4cab-62b6-49d1-b92d-102a4aed9d13">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 reiterates that the death benefit is payable only after the second insured dies, directly supporting and confirming the condition stated in fact sentence 1.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">1.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="f8fdda5e-551c-4714-929d-a06d99b71f24">
  <data key="d3">The requirement that the death benefit be paid only after the second insured's death does not affect the Trust’s authority to take policy loans, as loan rights stem from ownership rather than payout triggers.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="cd2b5513-fe2d-4b70-b48a-c2748515cd09">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 sets the default rule requiring the second death to trigger payment, but fact_sentence_2 modifies this by specifying immediate payout when both deaths occur simultaneously, thus creating a critical exception under ambiguous circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXCEPTION_TO_RULE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="a08544cf-1d57-4a48-8d71-04334028f70e">
  <data key="d3">The survivorship payout condition ensures the death benefit is deferred until both insureds pass, which complements the irrevocable trust ownership designed to remove assets from taxable estates. This structural alignment supports long-term wealth transfer goals without premature distribution.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="09580835-e917-49b8-9fa3-3cd68120d06a">
  <data key="d3">The survivorship payout feature ensures the death benefit is deferred until both insureds pass, making it ideal for estate planning purposes where timing matters. Holding the policy in an irrevocable trust allows exclusion from taxable estates, leveraging the delayed payout to achieve tax efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="115deb5a-9af7-4024-bf11-8dba457cef0b">
  <data key="d3">The survival-based payout mechanism ensures the death benefit aligns with estate planning goals managed through the trust, while the trust's assignment power allows flexible management of the policy outside standard beneficiary rules.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7" target="8a12f494-7f87-46bf-8e19-a11973792e7f">
  <data key="d3">The Trust's right to surrender the policy allows it to terminate coverage before any claim is made, potentially preventing the eventual death benefit payout under fact_sentence_1. This gives the Trust significant control over whether the policy ever results in a payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="47a324cb-869a-4639-9983-ecdb438b62fd" target="c77b94e8-ba17-40c7-a87e-8036edb72fd7">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">governs</data>
</edge>
