  <data key="d3">The grace period allows time to pay overdue premiums without immediate cancellation, preserving the policyholder’s ability to later access loans even after the first death.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="0df8a60d-2dd5-49e0-a026-86763abbc127">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a grace period allows time to pay overdue premiums without immediate cancellation, while the prohibition on partial withdrawals limits how policyholders may access accumulated cash value during such periods.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO DIRECT RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="a9741679-16e2-4128-9ef2-70da1404cc14">
  <data key="d3">The grace period allows time to pay overdue premiums without immediate cancellation, preserving the policy’s status so that options like splitting remain viable after the first death.</data>
  <data key="d4">PRECEDES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="80ae385b-ee9d-4149-9014-5ca0ab9ab203">
  <data key="d3">A 60-day grace period allows delayed premium payments without immediate cancellation, preserving policy status. This could enable continuation of coverage until the two-year window for splitting the policy begins after the first death.</data>
  <data key="d4">PRECEDES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="c0978b9e-d3be-4833-93c7-b90a6f87d86b">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a 60-day grace period allows continued policy validity despite late payments, ensuring eligibility for potential post-first-death policy splits. This supports the conditions under which actuarial allocation determines each new policy's face amount.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="6423aae2-3458-45ed-a68e-9f42cae005bf">
  <data key="d3">The grace period allows delayed payment without immediate cancellation, but it has no bearing on the eligibility criteria for splitting the policy under exceptional circumstances such as uncertain simultaneous deaths.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="017f6bf7-89af-44fe-8781-e25d1c431f9c">
  <data key="d3">The grace period ensures that failure to pay promptly does not immediately terminate coverage, preserving the potential for the policy to remain active long enough for the split provision to become relevant upon the first death.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDIRECTLY SUPPORTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.35</data>
</edge>
<edge source="3e0929d5-298d-4dde-8125-af7d9ada617a" target="47f15981-6589-47fa-8b46-dc246096d7cb">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a 60-day grace period ensures that missed payments don’t immediately cancel coverage, maintaining policy stability. This operational detail does not influence how often the split option is utilized.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
