<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="58b1aeda-54dc-45c2-bbb1-4a25c293a831">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">DATE</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="00066f02-a38b-49d1-abda-4a4d40847e5c">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization before assigning the policy supports the condition that only those explicitly named in the trust document hold rightful ownership, ensuring alignment with the trust's stipulations.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENFORCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="53c06c8c-e397-4baa-9acf-75ee3c5472d5">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization before assigning the policy ensures that transfer controls remain intact. This condition operates independently of the policyholder’s ability to cancel the policy within the free look period.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="510a4351-8c5a-44d4-a731-1c1dea4323a8">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization before assigning the policy does not affect the availability of a 30-day free look period for cancellation and refund. These provisions address separate procedural aspects of policy management.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="a81b97c9-146b-4343-9c7c-db9ffafb1f05">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence outlines a procedural requirement regarding policy assignment, while the second identifies the geographic base of the insurer. These statements address different aspects—governance rules versus organizational logistics—with no mutual dependence.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="4bed57f0-9e45-43f0-a325-58c36ead1450">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence outlines procedural constraints on policy transfer requiring trustee consent. The second sentence identifies a leadership role within the issuing company, offering contextual information about the organization's hierarchy but unrelated to the policy’s assignability.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="d1560b93-8e33-4768-9496-b61910e313b4">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization before assigning the policy exists independently of when the document was issued. The issuance date provides context but does not affect the validity or applicability of the assignment clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="2c0afdf7-25f1-4c84-88a2-08afcc4de210">
  <data key="d3">The rule requiring trustee authorization for assignment reinforces the legitimacy of the trust’s exclusive ownership, ensuring no unauthorized changes occur to the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="288469c9-a76a-4bf8-b17b-752662f3a1f6">
  <data key="d3">The restriction on assigning the policy without trustee approval ensures the trust maintains exclusive control, supporting its status as both owner and beneficiary.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="eb6fcda1-6a00-45e2-a278-6ecbfce50c86">
  <data key="d3">The rule prohibiting unapproved policy assignments depends on having an officially appointed trustee, such as First National Bank of Connecticut, whose authorization is required—a detail confirmed by the first fact sentence.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENFORCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="5c3f4413-4b34-4cda-af56-ca0ece4739ca">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization before assigning the policy underscores why the policy’s issuance date matters, as it marks the start of enforceable terms governing its transferability.</data>
  <data key="d4">TIMING_DEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="5f7b886a-0895-4321-a983-5dcc79a03f70">
  <data key="d3">The restriction on assigning the policy without trustee consent supports its function in estate planning by maintaining control over beneficiary distribution and preserving intended wealth transfer mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="70d6f164-2c6e-4960-9154-c1e13245d12c">
  <data key="d3">Restrictions on assigning the policy support its role in providing estate tax liquidity by preventing premature transfers that could compromise estate planning objectives.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENHANCES_PURPOSE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="30aa32c7-4f8f-42cf-99bf-592ed9d2eaab">
  <data key="d3">Restrictions on transferring the policy without trustee consent (fact_sentence_2) apply regardless of whether the death benefit has been triggered, meaning such limitations remain relevant even after both insured persons pass away, potentially affecting who receives the $2.5M payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="b0f11a92-dc8a-424a-9605-d53beb6ddfba">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for trustee authorization to assign the policy logically follows from the absence of individual ownership rights established in the first fact.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="e3e49333-4660-4170-be0e-750c514619c6">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 emphasizes that assignment is impossible without trustee permission, supporting and clarifying the prerequisite outlined in fact_sentence_1 regarding authorized transfers under trust governance.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="a08544cf-1d57-4a48-8d71-04334028f70e">
  <data key="d3">The need for trustee authorization to assign the policy stems from the fact that ownership lies exclusively with the irrevocable trust, ensuring that only authorized actions are taken under trust governance.</data>
  <data key="d4">JUSTIFIED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="a9741679-16e2-4128-9ef2-70da1404cc14">
  <data key="d3">Trustee authorization required for any assignment limits the flexibility to restructure the policy, including splitting it into two single-life policies after the first death, thereby constraining execution of the split provision.</data>
  <data key="d4">RESTRICTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="80ae385b-ee9d-4149-9014-5ca0ab9ab203">
  <data key="d3">Restrictions on assigning the policy without trustee consent support the controlled environment needed when splitting the policy post-first-death, particularly in complex estate arrangements.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLIES_WITH</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="017f6bf7-89af-44fe-8781-e25d1c431f9c">
  <data key="d3">The need for trustee authorization before assigning the policy reinforces the importance of maintaining compliance with trust terms when triggering events such as splitting the policy after the first death, ensuring alignment with estate plan objectives.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="eead227f-2ad6-4a45-877d-62ba5081b981">
  <data key="d3">Restrictions on policy assignment without trustee consent support the conditionality around splitting the policy, ensuring only authorized parties can initiate such actions even when insurability criteria are met.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="dc5e7101-a50d-4b25-8d7d-9d2fc8fab9fc" target="3438d3e0-d9fb-46af-8d4c-8f979cc55d1f">
  <data key="d3">Trustee authorization requirements ensure that any structural change, such as policy splitting for divorce or trust updates, remains compliant with established trust terms and governance protocols.</data>
  <data key="d4">REQUIRED_FOR</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
